{"description":"M3a — eka verify --changed: diff, resolve, run the checks a standard declares, and report a violation the way a failing test reports, carrying the constraint id and the location. This is the answer to 'the agent does not always hold the standard' that does not depend on the agent reading anything. Stage (a) is repository-scope and needs no binding: run every check a standard declares for this repository. Stage (b) becomes path-scope once M1's appliesTo exists. Install it at the git hook and in CI, because git is the one interface every host passes through; a host-specific pre-tool hook (for example Claude's PreToolUse) may be added later as a sharper layer, never as the substitute. Default non-blocking for warnings and blocking only for definitive violations, with --force for a deliberate violation, so the gate does not become friction that is skipped with --no-verify.","acceptanceCriteria":"- eka verify --changed runs the repository-scope checks declared by standards and exits 1 on a definitive violation, 0 otherwise; warnings do not block.\n- A violation prints the constraint id, the file and line, and what is required, in a form an agent can act on without further retrieval, plus the exact eka get command for the constraint.\n- --json emits a deterministic report naming each check, its verdict, and the constraint id.\n- The pre-commit and pre-push hook templates call it, remaining non-blocking by default so the capture contract is preserved; CI wiring lands in the same change.\n- A test proves non-vacuity: a repository with a declared check and a violating change exits 1, and the same change with a warning-level check exits 0.\n- Timing: the repository-scope stage must stay in the seconds range on the eka repository itself, measured and recorded, because a slow gate is a skipped gate."}